To terminate a C program, you can use the standard library function exit(). This function takes an integer argument that specifies the exit status of the program. A value of 0 typically indicates success, while non-zero values indicate an error condition. Here is an example:

#include <stdlib.h>
int main() {
// Program logic here
exit(0);
}


In this example, the program will exit with a status of 0, indicating success. However, it's important to note that the exit function should only be used in certain situations, such as when all necessary cleanup has been performed and the program is ready to terminate. It should not be used as a replacement for proper error handling and exception handling mechanisms in your program. Instead, consider using these mechanisms to gracefully handle errors and provide a better user experience.

